Unrelated environment/pre-existing finding (NOT caused by NEARS-3643's diff; location_controller.dart
and auth_controller.dart/auth_repository.dart are untouched by this ticket):

Firebase.initializeApp() fails for this worktree's suffixed package id
(com.izzes.nears.nears_nears_3643_help_chat_redesign) — logged every boot as:
  [FAIL] type=FirebaseInitFailure msg="firebase init failed (PlatformException)"

Because main.dart's telemetry-setup try/catch wraps the PlatformDispatcher.instance.onError
wiring itself, that Firebase failure causes the wiring to be skipped entirely, so a SUBSEQUENT
synchronous throw from any bare (unawaited) `FirebaseMessaging.instance` call is never routed
through the app's own handleUncaughtAsyncError/AppLogger pipeline — it surfaces only as a raw
VM-level "Unhandled Exception" print.

Two concrete call sites hit live during this QA run:
1. LocationController._saveDataAndFirebaseConfig -> LocationService.configureFirebaseMessaging
   (location_service.dart:151) — a bare (non-awaited) call. Because it's a synchronous throw
   inside an async function body with no try/catch, execution of the REST of
   _saveDataAndFirebaseConfig aborts: AddressHelper.saveUserAddressInSharedPref(address) and
   HomeController.loadData(true)/routeToDestination(...) never run. Net effect: picking a NEW
   location as a guest (via Search Location or Use current location -> Pick Location) silently
   never completes/navigates — the user is stuck on pick_map_screen with no error toast, no
   AppLogger line reaching the app's own failure pipeline (only the raw VM print).
2. AuthRepository.updateToken (auth_repository.dart:256), reached via
   AuthService.login -> AuthController.login -> AuthController.validateAndLogin ->
   SignInView._login. AuthController.login's own try/catch DOES catch+rethrow (sets
   _errorMessage, calls update()), but validateAndLogin's `await login(...)` is unguarded, so
   the exception propagates further and becomes another unhandled top-level Future error.
   Because AuthRepository.saveUserToken(...) runs (and succeeds) BEFORE the crashing
   updateToken() call, the auth token IS actually persisted — so a subsequent app relaunch
   (am start, no reinstall) DOES land the user logged in, masking the crash's effect on this
   particular path. clearSharedPrefGuestId() never runs, though.

Workaround used to complete this QA run (read-only on the DB, no product code touched): wrote a
minimal valid AddressModel JSON directly into SharedPreferences (flutter.6ammart_user_address,
zone 1 "Main Service Zone") via `run-as ... shared_prefs/FlutterSharedPreferences.xml`, so splash
routing (route_helper.dart:1462, address-present skips AccessLocationScreen) reached Home without
re-entering the crashing code path.

Recommend: wrap configureFirebaseMessaging's call site (or the function itself) in try/catch (it
already has zero test coverage for the Firebase-absent case), same treatment other Firebase call
sites in this codebase already get. Filed as a non-blocking regression-candidate followup, not a
NEARS-3643 task_bug — the touched files are unrelated to this ticket's diff.
